  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Vaccines are safe, effective biological preparations that provide active acquired immunity against infectious diseases by training the immune system to recognize and fight pathogens. They protect against numerous diseases, including COVID-19, influenza, measles, and HPV, and are crucial for preventing severe illness across all ages. Key Vaccine Information How They Work: Vaccines stimulate the immune system to create antibodies without causing the disease itself. Types: Types include mRNA (e.g., COVID-19), viral vector, and toxoid vaccines. Schedules: Vaccines are recommended from infancy (e.g., DTaP, Hib, Polio) through adulthood (e.g., Flu, Tdap, Shingles) to maintain immunity. Safety: Vaccines undergo extensive testing and monitoring to ensure they are safe. Access: In California, residents can find, book, and track vaccinations via My Turn. My Turn Commonly Recommended Vaccines Infants/Children: DTaP (diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis), Hib, Hepatitis B, Polio, Rotavirus, Measles-Mumps-Rubella (MMR). Adults: Annual flu vaccine, COVID-19, Tdap (tetanus, diphtheria, pertussis), and shingles (for older adults). Newer Developments: Emerging vaccines target Lyme disease, showing roughly 75% efficacy in trials. For personalized vaccine schedules and to find locations, you can consult official resources like the CDC vaccine schedules or Immunize.org. CDC vaccine schedules Immunize.org",
